  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires finding a step function h(x) on [0,2] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum correlation with its reflected version. The approach uses a multi-stage optimization:\n   - Iterative smoothing and refinement through gradient-based optimization\n   - Successive refinement with hierarchical representations (step functions, smooth functions) allows exploiting structures at different scales\n   - Entropic regularization in the smooth phase provides convexity and gradient information\n   - The hierarchical transfer mechanism maintains precision while allowing optimization at different resolutions\n   - The adaptive budget allocation ensures efficient use of running time by focusing computation where it might yield the most improvement\n\n   Key limitations addressed:\n   - The previous method relied too heavily on local optimization and random restarts\n   - We add structured multi-scale analysis to better navigate the complex solution space\n   - The hierarchical approach allows escaping local optima through scale transitions\n   - Entropic regularization is added to create smoother objectives with better derivatives\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   - Representation: Discrete uniform grids starting from coarse representations (e.g., 25 points) and interpolated to the full parent resolution (200 points)\n   - Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k [\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx]\n   - Primary constraint: \u222b\u2080\u00b2h(x)dx = 1\n   - Smooth Optimization Phase: Minimize \u03b5-standardized cross-correlation energy with projected entropic term\n   - Refinement Phase: Non-smooth projected gradient descent on the original objective\n   - Cross-scale Transfer: Bi-criteria interpolation preserving integral constraint and feature positioning\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   Pre-Imported Functions:\n   project_feasible(h, n): Maintains integrality and clipping to [0,1]\n   compute_c5(h, n): Evaluates correlation maximum scaled by dx\n\n   Stages:\n   a. Coarse initialization: Start with lower-resolution grid from parent\n   b. Smooth optimization: Use entropy-regularized risk minimization\n   c. Scale transfer: Non-linear interpolation while preserving integral\n   d. Projected gradient refinement: Uncoregularized optimization\n   e. Budget allocation: Sequential phases with time horizon monitoring\n   f. Local search: Small perturbations of promising configurations\n\n   Workflow:\n   ```\n   deadline = t0 + budget_s - 20\n   best = None\n\n   if initial_h_values exists:\n     base = initial_h_values.copy()\n   else:\n     base = None\n\n   n0 = choose_start_resolution()\n   h0 = initialize_from_parent(base, n0)\n   h0_proj = project_feasible(h0, n0)\n\n   Phase 1 - Entropic Smoothing (budget ~ 40%):\n     alpha = 3e6\n     h_smooth = entropic_optimize(h0_proj, n0, alpha, deadline)\n     c_smooth = compute_c5(h_smooth, n0)\n\n   Phase 2 - Scale Transfer (budget ~ 30%):\n     scaled = transfer_resolution(h_smooth, n0, deadline)\n     c_spatial = compute_c5(scaled_full, 200)\n     best = (scaled_full, c_spatial, 200)\n\n   Phase 3 - Projection Adjustment (budget ~ 20%):\n     final = projected_refine(best_guess, deadline)\n     c_final = compute_c5(final, 200)\n   ```\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   - Primary optimization method: SciPy's SLSQP with adaptive alpha-heuristic\n   - Resolution progression: 50, 100, 150, 200 points\n   - Alpha sequence: 1e6 to 1e3 with log-spaced scaling\n   - Perturbation budget: Up to 5% of grid points modified at each restart\n   - Dimension selection: Initial grids chosen as powers of 2 (50, 100, 160)\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   - Parition compute into three phases with 40-30-20 allocation\n   - Each phase has strict time checks and early termination\n   - Fallback mechanism: If early termination, revert to last valid solution\n   - Dimension handling: Explicit grid interpolation preserving integral/positions\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   - Feasibility: clip [0,1] and verify integral=1\n   - O(n\u2080) operation for validation\n   - Return (h_full, C\u2085_full, n_points=200)\n   - Consistency check: reevaluate C\u2085 for final candidate\n   - NaN detection: Skip invalid candidates automatically",
